# DURABLE REPRO — A1: calling a `Callable`-typed LOCAL bound to an `&`-param
# function SEGFAULTS.
#
# ⚠ READ THE FILENAME CAREFULLY. `_amp_param_` names the CALLEE's `&`-parameter
# signature (`void bumpint(int &n)`), NOT how the callable is bound: the
# callable here is a **LOCAL**, declared inside `main`. Its sibling
# `sound_callable_amp_param_projection.gg` is the one where the callable arrives
# as a PARAMETER. The two filenames read as a matched pair and are not one.
#
# WRONG TODAY (measured at HEAD, Rust gg, C backend): `gg check` reports "OK: no
# semantic errors", `gg build` succeeds, and the binary exits **139 (SIGSEGV)**.
# Bare `f(x)` crashes the same way; a plain-arg `Callable[void(int)]` works, and
# the assignment on its own works — so the defect is specifically the `&`-param
# signature reached through an INDIRECT call.
#
# INTENDED: the indirect call is an ordinary write-through call. `bumpint`
# increments the caller's `x`, so the program prints 42 and exits 0. (If the
# language instead decides an `&`-param function may not be reified into a
# `Callable` value at all, the reference-grade answer is a CHECK-TIME rejection —
# never an accepted program that segfaults. Either way today's behaviour is a
# defect; this fixture asserts the accept-and-run reading, which is what the
# `Callable[void(&int)]` type annotation the compiler already accepts promises.)
#
# The seed is computed, not a literal, so no const-fold can elide the call.
#
#
# ORACLE (ggdef, measured this session): OUT OF SUBSET — "unresolved local
# `bumpint`"; ggdef does not reify a named function into a `Callable` value.
# Recorded explicitly per Core #9.
# Cited from TODO.md (CoW / ownership / High): "Calling a `Callable`-typed LOCAL
# bound to an `&`-param function SEGFAULTS".
int seed():
    return 41

void bumpint(int &n):
    n = n + 1

void main():
    int x = seed()
    Callable[void(&int)] f = bumpint
    f(&x)
    print(x)
